" (https://www.gov.ca.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/FINAL-N-9-26-AI-EO-9.18.26-SIGNED.pdf)\nciteturn980view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 3; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn979view0\",\"lineno\":50}); Total lines: 129\nL0@P0: EXECUTIVE DEPARTMENT\nL1@P0: STATE OF CALIFORNIA\nL2@P0: EXECUTIVE ORDER N-9-26\nL3@P0: WHEREAS California is the largest economy in the nation, the world’s\nL4@P0: preeminent innovation ecosystem, and an incubator for new technologies, as\nL5@P0: well as the businesses that create and scale those technologies; and\nL6@P0: WHEREAS California dominates AI innovation, with 32 of the top 50 private\nL7@P0: AI companies in the world based in California, even as no state has taken more\nL8@P0: aggressive action to strengthen the safety, security, and consumer privacy of\nL9@P0: technology and online platforms, including AI; and\nL10@P0: WHEREAS, in the face of inaction by Congress, California has also led the\nL11@P0: nation by enacting laws and taking executive action addressing frontier AI trust\nL12@P0: and safety, children's safety online, data privacy, cybersecurity, and consumer\nL13@P0: protection, proving that innovation and economic growth are fully compatible\nL14@P0: with common sense regulations that address risks and advance public safety\nL15@P0: through responsible deployment of AI systems and models; and\nL16@P0: WHEREAS on September 6, 2023, I issued Executive Order N-12-23 directing\nL17@P0: how the State would safely innovate to use AI for the benefit of Californians, as\nL18@P0: well as identify potential risks to individuals, communities, state government, and\nL19@P0: state workers; and\nL20@P0: WHEREAS in response to Executive Order N-12-23, my Administration\nL21@P0: established guidelines for public sector procurement of AI products, uses, and\nL22@P0: required trainings; published guidelines for State agencies and departments to\nL23@P0: analyze the impact of AI tools on marginalized and vulnerable communities;\nL24@P0: published reports on building and supporting an AI-ready state workforce; and\nL25@P0: launched pilots of AI projects to help improve public services; and\nL26@P0: WHEREAS in 2024, I convened a group of world-leading AI researchers and\nL27@P0: experts, who released a first-in-the-nation report on frontier AI guardrails, based\nL28@P0: on empirical, science-based analysis of the capabilities and attendant risks of\nL29@P0: frontier models, including recommendations on ensuring evidence-based\nL30@P0: policymaking, balancing the need for transparency, safety, and trust with\nL31@P0: innovation, and the group released its report in June 2025; and\nL32@P0: WHEREAS in 2025, in partnership with the Legislature, I signed first-in-the\u0002nation legislation, Senate Bill 53, addressing frontier AI trust and safety, which\nL33@P0: built on the recommendations included in the report released by the AI\nL34@P0: researchers and experts earlier that year and established baseline requirements\nL35@P0: for deploying safe, secure, and trustworthy artificial intelligence, which took\nL36@P0: effect this year; and\nL37@P0: WHEREAS this nation-leading legislation, along with other legislation that, in\nL38@P0: partnership with the Legislature, I have signed addressing children's safety\nL39@P0: online, data privacy, cybersecurity, and consumer protection, proves that\nL40@P0: innovation and economic growth are fully compatible with appropriate\nL41@P0: guardrails; and\nL42@P0: WHEREAS on March 30, 2026, I issued Executive Order N-5-26 directing my\nL43@P0: Administration to ensure AI procurement and adoption protect civil rights, civil\nL44@P0: liberties, and privacy, while reinforcing the importance of transparent, safe, and\nL45@P0: responsible AI adoption to make government more efficient, effective, and\nL46@P0-1: engaged; andWHEREAS earlier this month, I signed legislation that establishes a first-in\u0002the-nation framework for certifying independent verification organizations, with\nL47@P1: sufficient technical expertise to objectively review AI models and systems for\nL48@P1: safety risks and make recommendations to address identified risks and\nL49@P1: regulating AI auditors through independence, transparency, and integrity\nL50@P1: standards; and\nL51@P1: WHEREAS my recent actions occurred against the backdrop of revelations\nL52@P1: of multiple instances of apparent attempts by individuals to use AI products to\nL53@P1: create bioweapons and AI agents working, at times independently and at times\nL54@P1: collectively, to defeat security protocols that AI companies had put in place\nL55@P1: and working, in some instances undetected for months, to hack other\nL56@P1: companies, and other behavior that raises grave concerns about potential\nL57@P1: threats to public safety; and\nL58@P1: WHEREAS these revelations have prompted many Americans and some\nL59@P1: within the AI industry, including company leaders, to call on the industry to pace\nL60@P1: the development of AI systems and models and invite more stringent regulations\nL61@P1: by government to address security and safety risks; and\nL62@P1: WHEREAS California must continue to lead, both in supporting responsible\nL63@P1: growth of emerging technologies and addressing their impacts on society and\nL64@P1: maintaining economic opportunity for all Californians, including by reviewing\nL65@P1: and updating, as needed, existing protections and exploring other tools for\nL66@P1: more broadly sharing the economic benefits with workers in industries and\nL67@P1: businesses impacted by AI and other emerging technologies; and\nL68@P1: WHEREAS it is in our collective interest to respond swiftly to the recent\nL69@P1: developments that highlight emerging risks and the need for additional\nL70@P1: safeguards around the deployment of AI models; and\nL71@P1: WHEREAS, absent federal action—which unfortunately is not forthcoming\nL72@P1: due to a failure of leadership by the President and Congressional leaders—\nL73@P1: California will continue to lead by taking action to swiftly implement our existing\nL74@P1: laws around AI security and risks and develop a roadmap for further\nL75@P1: strengthening our nation-leading laws to respond to these newly emerging risks.\nL76@P1: NOW, THEREFORE, I, GAVIN NEWSOM, Governor of the State of California,\nL77@P1: in accordance with the authority vested in me by the State Constitution and\nL78@P1: statutes of the State of California, do hereby issue the following Order to\nL79@P1: become effective immediately:\nL80@P1: IT IS HEREBY ORDERED THAT:\nL81@P1: 1. No later than May 1, 2027, the Government Operations Agency shall\nL82@P1: complete the requirements of Section 8898.1 of the Government Code\nL83@P1: and develop application requirements, procedures, and criteria for\nL84@P1: independent verification organizations and publicly post them, as\nL85@P1: required by law.\nL86@P1: 2. No later than December 1, 2027, the Government Operations Agency\nL87@P1: shall complete the requirements of subdivision (a) of Section 11549.82 of\nL88@P1: the Government Code and begin taking the actions required by\nL89@P1: subdivision (b) of that Section.\nL90@P1: \nL91@P1: 3. The Government Operations Agency, in consultation with the Governor’s\nL92@P1: Office of Emergency Services, shall, no later than November 16, 2026,\nL93@P1-2: submit to my office recommendations, developed in consultation with national experts, addressing the technical feasibility and potential\nL94@P2: efficacy of amendments to existing state laws regarding AI safety and\nL95@P2: security, including at least the following:\nL96@P2: a. Requiring that all large frontier developers embed designated\nL97@P2: independent verification organizations onsite in their labs to\nL98@P2: conduct periodic audits and evaluations.\nL99@P2: b. Requiring that the safety frameworks, transparency reports, and risk\nL100@P2: assessments that frontier AI companies are required to file be\nL101@P2: independently verified pursuant to standards determined to be\nL102@P2: adequate by an independent verification organization.\nL103@P2: c. Requiring the creation of a “kill switch” for frontier models, with the\nL104@P2: efficacy of the switch verified on an ongoing basis by an\nL105@P2: independent verification organization.\nL106@P2: d. Updating the definition of critical safety incidents that AI companies\nL107@P2: are required to report to include a range of loss-of-control incidents,\nL108@P2: covering recently reported incidents from large frontier developers.\nL109@P2: I FURTHER DIRECT that as soon as hereafter possible, this Order be filed in\nL110@P2: the Office of the Secretary of State and that widespread publicity and notice\nL111@P2: be given of this Order.\nL112@P2: This Order is not intended to, and does not, create any rights or benefits,\nL113@P2: substantive or procedural, enforceable at law or in equity, against the State of\nL114@P2: California, its agencies, departments, entities, officers, employees, or any other\nL115@P2: person.\nL116@P2:  IN WITNESS WHEREOF I have\nL117@P2:  hereunto set my hand and caused\nL118@P2: the Great Seal of the State of\nL119@P2: California to be affixed this 18th day\nL120@P2:  of September 2026.\nL121@P2: \nL122@P2:  _________________________\nL123@P2:  GAVIN NEWSOM\nL124@P2: Governor of California\nL125@P2:  ATTEST:\nL126@P2:  _____________________________\nL127@P2: SHIRLEY N. WEBER, PH. D\nL128@P2: Secretary of State\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGovernor Newsom issues executive order to accelerate independent oversight and advance the creation of an AI kill switch | Governor of California (https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/18/governor-newsom-issues-executive-order-to-accelerate-independent-oversight-and-advance-the-creation-of-an-ai-kill-switch/)\nciteturn980view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn979view1\",\"lineno\":40}); Total lines: 129\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Governor of California L3: \nL4:   * cite2†About L5:   * cite3†Priorities L6:   * cite4†News L7:   * cite5†Join the Administration L8:   * cite6†First Partner L9:   * cite7†Contact L10:   * To search the site, enter a search term [Input]\nL11: \nL12:   * Official website of the State of California\nL13: \nL14: cite8†CA.gov†www.ca.gov L15: \nL16: Resources for California\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19:   * Key services\nL20:   * cite9†Health insurance or Medi-Cal†www.coveredca.com L21:   * cite10†Business licenses†www.cdtfa.ca.gov L22:   * cite11†Food & social assistance†benefitscal.com L23:   * cite12†Find a CA state job†calcareers.ca.gov L24:   * cite13†Vehicle registration†www.dmv.ca.gov L25:   * [Button: More +]\nL26:   * cite14†Digital vaccine record†myvaccinerecord.cdph.ca.gov L27:   * cite15†Traffic tickets†www.ca.gov L28:   * cite16†Birth certificates†www.cdph.ca.gov L29:   * cite17†Lottery numbers†www.calottery.com L30:   * cite18†Unemployment†edd.ca.gov L31:   * cite19†View all CA.gov services†www.ca.gov L32:   * [Button: Less -]\nL33: \nL34: * * *\nL35:   * Popular topics\nL36:   * cite20†Abortion†abortion.ca.gov L37:   * cite21†Building California†build.ca.gov L38:   * cite22†Climate Action†climateaction.ca.gov L39:   * cite23†Gun Safety†www.caloes.ca.gov L40:   * cite24†Mental health care for all†mentalhealth.ca.gov L41:   * cite25†Opioids†www.cdph.ca.gov L42: \nL43: * * *\nL44: \nL45: Back to top\nL46: \nL47: Sep 18, 2026\nL48: # Governor Newsom issues executive order to accelerate independent oversight and advance the creation of an AI kill switch\nL49: #### Calls for a national adoption of California’s AI framework\nL50: \nL51: What you need to know: Governor Newsom today issued an cite26†executive order directing actions to address the dangers of recent AI incidents. The order accelerates California’s new law establishing first-in-the-nation independent oversight of AI companies and safety checks and advances the creation of an “AI kill switch.”\nL52: SACRAMENTO – With Donald Trump and Congress asleep at the wheel, Governor Gavin Newsom is once again taking the lead to strengthen AI safety for all Americans. Today, the Governor issued an cite26†executive order to dramatically accelerate the implementation of new third-party oversight of safety and security risks in AI systems, as well as independent audits.\nL53: The executive order convenes a group of world-leading experts to provide, within two months, a guide for California to reinforce and strengthen its AI safety and security laws. Proposals under consideration include requiring independent third parties to write safety plans for frontier AI companies, as well as requiring companies to develop an emergency shutoff, or “kill switch,” for frontier models.\nL54: California leads the nation in oversight of emerging frontier technologies, with laws addressing nearly every major area of AI policy: frontier-model safety, independent oversight, children and companion chatbots, privacy, deepfakes, fraud, cybersecurity, workers and government deployment.\nL55: \nL56: The federal government’s abject failure to create any form of meaningful AI oversight or accountability should alarm every American, especially when AI CEOs themselves are begging for regulation.\nL57: We’re not waiting to act – we’re going to speed up our work on substantial and responsible AI oversight before it’s too late. We’re going to do this thoughtfully but with urgent velocity; the stakes are too high to wait or delay action.\nL58: \nL59: While Washington abdicates its responsibility to protect Americans, California is building on the strongest AI regulatory framework in the nation. California has already built a national model, and our policy should be the national baseline.\nL60: \nL61: Governor Gavin Newsom\nL62: The executive order comes after recent alarming incidents, including the Hugging Face attack, prompting many Americans, including leaders in the AI industry, to call on the industry to pace the development of AI systems and models and push for more stringent government regulations to address security and safety risks. In response, the federal government has done nothing.\nL63: No federal law requires AI companies to report dangerous incidents when they happen, and President Trump has rejected calls for new regulations that would increase security and protect the health, safety, and well-being of the American people.\nL64: cite26†Read the executive order here .\nL65: ## Decisive action to strengthen AI oversight\nL66: California provides a model for national standards around AI safety. Last week, Governor Newsom signed legislation creating first-in-the-nation safeguards and accountability measures to strengthen California’s nation-leading framework. The Governor signed into law Senate Bill 813 (McNerney),  establishing a framework for independent verification organizations that can assess AI systems and models for safety and risk.\nL67: Governor Newsom also signed AB 1405 (Bauer-Kahan), creating a state registry for AI auditors that assess AI systems and models for compliance with state law and establishing standards for their independence, transparency, and integrity.\nL68: Today’s executive order takes these actions even further. Through the order, the Governor is directing the Government Operations Agency: to accelerate the implementation timelines for SB 813 and AB 1405, and, in consultation with the Governor’s Office of Emergency Services, to convene national experts to develop recommendations for potential changes to further strengthen state law to:\nL69:   * Require frontier AI companies to embed a designated independent verification organization onsite in their labs to conduct regular audits and evaluations.\nL70:   * Require that the AI safety frameworks, transparency reports, and risk assessments that frontier AI companies are required to file under state law are verified pursuant to standards deemed adequate by an independent verification organization.\nL71:   * Advance the creation of a “kill switch” for frontier models, with the efficacy of the switch verified on an ongoing basis by an independent verification organization.\nL72:   * Update definitions of critical safety incidents to include loss-of-control incidents such as the Hugging Face attack.\nL73: ## An AI safety model for the nation\nL74: \nL75: Under Governor Newsom’s leadership, California is already far ahead of every other state in regulating AI, creating a guidebook for the nation. The Governor is calling on Congress and President Trump to review and adopt the state’s framework, or use its nation-leading regulation as a floor, not a ceiling, for the benefit and safety of all Americans.\nL76: ## California’s nation-leading framework\nL77: \nL78: Beginning with his cite27†first-in-nation 2023 executive order†us.list-manage.com on generative AI, Governor Newsom has pursued executive action, legislation passed in partnership with the Legislature, regulation, procurement, and public-private partnerships to address nearly every major area of AI policy:\nL79: ✅ Frontier AI safety: In 2025, Governor Newsom signed SB 53, the cite28†Transparency in Frontier Artificial Intelligence Act , the nation’s first AI safety law. It requires frontier AI developers to publicly disclose safety frameworks, report specified critical safety incidents to the state, and protect whistleblowers who report serious risks.\nL80: ✅ Independent AI oversight: In 2026, Governor Newsom signed SB 813, making California the cite29†first state in the nation to establish a framework for certifying independent verification organizations with sufficient expertise and demonstrated independence from AI companies to objectively assess AI systems and models for safety and risk. He also signed AB 1405, establishing a state registry for AI auditors and standards for their independence, transparency and integrity.\nL81: ✅ Child safety standards for AI companions: This year, Governor Newsom signed the bipartisan Adam’s Law, cite30†requiring safeguards around companion chatbots used by children, including suicide and self-harm crisis protocols, parental controls and notifications when safety settings are disabled. It is the first law in the country requiring independent child-safety audits and comprehensive risk assessments prior to release.\nL82: California also enacted laws to ban toys containing AI companion chatbots for five years; prohibit use of student personal information to train AI models; and expand protections against AI-generated child sexual abuse material.\nL83: ✅ Social media and children: Governor Newsom signed the strongest-in-nation legislation prohibiting social-media companies from providing users under 16 with cite30†addictive features including autoplay and algorithmic feeds based on user history and profiles.\nL84: California has enacted nation-leading requirements for responsible AI and social media use for children, including cite31†warning labels for young users, required cite31†age verification signals , guidance to protect kids from after-school cyberbullying, and prohibiting online platforms from knowingly providing an addictive feed to a minor without parental consent.\nL85: ✅ Deep fakes and synthetic content: In nation-leading action, cite32†California banned and cracked down on sexually explicit deepfakes, required AI watermarking, addressed deceptive AI-generated election content and required transparency around AI training data.\nL86: ✅ Protecting privacy and civil liberties: Governor Newsom signed the Delete Act, cite33†a first-in-the-nation privacy law allowing Californians to direct registered data brokers to delete their personal information. California subsequently launched DROP, which enables people to submit a single deletion request to registered data brokers. In cite34†March 2026 , Governor Newsom also issued an executive order strengthening privacy and civil-liberties protections for state AI procurement.\nL87: CalPrivacy has separately adopted protections involving automated decision-making technology.\nL88: ✅ Workers and AI disruption: Governor Newsom signed a cite35†first-in-the-nation executive order addressing AI’s economic and labor impacts , including expanded tracking of AI’s economic effects and work to adapt job-training and public-benefit programs. California has also launched an AI Unemployment Tracker, and solicited statewide public input on AI’s economic impacts through Engaged California.\nL89: ✅ Cybersecurity: Governor Newsom launched a cite36†first-in-the-nation AI Cyber Defense Program to use AI to identify vulnerabilities , harden networks and support incident response. California is also establishing an AI Cybersecurity Officer in each agency and working with frontier AI companies and critical-infrastructure partners on response protocols and tabletop exercises.\nL90: ✅ AI-enabled fraud: Governor Newsom launched a cite37†Tech Fraud Task Force to combat AI-enabled fraud . California has also acted against AI-generated robocall scams and addressed algorithmic pricing through state antitrust law.\nL91: \nL92: cite38†Executive orders , cite39†Featured , cite40†Press releases , cite41†Public safety , cite42†Recent news , cite43†Top story L93: ## Recent news\nL94: \nL95: ## cite44†Governor Newsom issues legislative update 9.20.2026 L96: \nL97: Sep 20, 2026\nL98: \nL99: News SACRAMENTO – Governor Gavin Newsom today announced that he has signed the following bills:AB 28 by Assemblymember Pilar Schiavo (D-Santa Clarita) — Solid waste landfills: subsurface temperatures.AB 550 by Assemblymember Cottie Petrie-Norris (D-Irvine) — The...\nL100: ## cite45